## Issue File Format

**Agent's turn** (file starts with `go`):
```markdown
go
# [Issue Title]

Status: Planning | Development | Code Review | Development Complete | Complete
Tier: Trivial | Standard | Full
Mode: direct
Priority: High | Medium | Low
Branch: [link]
PR: [link]
Blocked:
Jira: https://preferredcredit.atlassian.net/browse/NGU-###
Jira Status: [verbatim Jira status name, refreshed by /work Phase 2]

## Discussion
_(Newest first - format: [agent] message)_

[agent] message...

## Description
[From Jira]
```

**Not agent's turn** (file starts with `#`):
```markdown
# [Issue Title]

Status: Planning | Development | Code Review | Development Complete | Complete
Tier: Trivial | Standard | Full
Mode: direct
...
```

**Field notes:**
- `Tier:` set during Phase 1 Triage. One of `Trivial` (skip spec + plan), `Standard` (skip spec, write plan), `Full` (write both). Locked once set unless explicitly retriaged.
- `Mode:` only ever `direct` or absent. `Mode: direct` means the issue is locked to a chat session opened via `/direct`; `/work` skips it. Absent = async-eligible.
- `Blocked:` empty when not blocked, contains reason when blocked.
- `Jira Status:` only on ticketed items (omit for adhoc).

## Two-Field Status Model

Local issue files have **two** status fields with very different ownership:

| Field | Owner | What it represents |
|-------|-------|--------------------|
| `Status:` | Claude / orchestrator / user (local) | Where the work is in the **local** workflow — drives what `/work` does next |
| `Jira Status:` | `/work` Phase 2 (read-only mirror of Jira) | The current Jira ticket status, refreshed every `/work` run |

**Why two fields:** "Code Review" locally means "agent finished, PR is up, awaiting human review." Jira's "Code Review" status is a separate, formal stage the user transitions manually after merging. These cadences naturally diverge — local progresses faster — and conflating them caused the local field to oscillate every time `/work` ran. The two fields are now decoupled.

**Rules:**
- Only Phase 2 of `/work` writes `Jira Status:`. Nothing else touches it.
- `Status:` is owned by whoever is currently driving the work (planning agent, dev agent, user). `/work` reads it to decide what to dispatch but never overwrites it from Jira.
- The dashboard shows both side by side; mismatch is normal (e.g., local `Code Review`, Jira `In Development` while the user reviews the PR before transitioning Jira).
- Sweep (move to `Complete\`) triggers on either: local `Status: Complete`, OR `Jira Status: Complete` / `Development Complete`. Either source declaring "done" removes it from `Active\`.

### `Tier:` and `Mode:` fields

`Tier:` and `Mode:` live alongside `Status:` but are owned independently. `Tier:` is set once during Triage and gates which phases run (see Workflow Phases). `Mode:` toggles whether the issue is locked to a chat session — `Mode: direct` means `/work` must skip it. See "Issue File Format" for the field shapes and lifecycle.

### Local `Status:` values (the ones Claude/`/work` cares about)

| Status | Meaning | `/work` Phase 5 action |
|--------|---------|------------------------|
| Planning | Claude researching/designing | Dispatch planning subagent |
| Development | Claude coding (branch exists) | Resume dev subagent |
| Code Review | PR created, waiting for human review | None (skip with warning if `go` set) |
| Development Complete | Local work done, awaiting Jira transition | None (skip with warning if `go` set) |
| Complete | Done locally — sweep eligible | Sweep, no dispatch |

`In Development` is also accepted as a synonym for `Development` (Jira's spelling). For pure parity, files may carry `QA`, `QA Complete`, or `Deployed` as local statuses, but `/work` treats them as non-actionable (same as `Code Review`).

### `Jira Status:` values

Whatever Jira says — passed through verbatim, including casing (e.g. `In Development`, `Development Complete`, `Ready for QA Testing`). Phase 2 normalises by trimming only.

## Workflow Phases

Both modes (`/work` async, `/direct` direct) drive the same phase sequence. Phase 0 setup always runs first; Phase 1 Triage determines tier; remaining phases run conditionally per tier.

### Phase 0: Setup

1. Folder created at `Active\<KEY>\` (or `Active\<slug>\`) if missing.
2. Jira Status refreshed (ticketed items only).
3. Shallow clone of required repos into `AgentWorkspace\` (only when Execute phase begins).
4. Branch `fb/<KEY>` created and pushed (only when Execute phase begins).

For ticketed items, if the folder doesn't exist, `/direct` auto-runs `/jira-import` to scaffold it.

### Phase 1: Triage

Claude reads the issue file plus the directly relevant code/spec and proposes one of three tiers:

| Tier | Spec | Plan | Examples |
|------|------|------|----------|
| **Trivial** | skip | skip | Version bump, rename, one-line fix, doc typo |
| **Standard** | skip | write `plan.md` | Few files, no architectural decisions, follows existing patterns |
| **Full** | write `spec.md` | write `plan.md` | New surface area, cross-repo, ambiguous requirements, design decisions |

In direct mode (`/direct`), the user confirms or overrides. In async mode (`/work`), the dispatched subagent commits the tier and logs it in Discussion. Tier is locked once written; user can retriage by editing the file and re-flagging `go`.

### Phase 2: Spec (Full tier only)

Invokes `superpowers:brainstorming`. Output saved to `Active\<KEY>\spec.md`. (The skill's default location is `docs/superpowers/specs/...`; override to the issue folder.)

In direct mode: spec is built interactively with the user.
In async mode: dispatched subagent builds spec autonomously and Discussion captures the summary.

### Phase 3: Plan (Standard + Full tiers)

Invokes `superpowers:writing-plans`. Output saved to `Active\<KEY>\plan.md`.

In direct mode: plan is shown to user for review.
In async mode: subagent finishes plan, sets `Status: Code Review`, removes `go` line — user reviews plan.md and re-adds `go` to approve.

### Phase 4: Execute (all tiers)

Direct mode: invokes `superpowers:subagent-driven-development`. Sub-subagents implement tasks; the chat session reviews between them.
Async mode: invokes `superpowers:executing-plans`. Dispatched subagent runs the plan with internal review checkpoints.

When Execute begins:
- AgentWorkspace clones happen if not already (Phase 0 deferred to first need).
- Branch `fb/<KEY>` created.
- Ticketed items get an auto Jira transition to "In Development" (see Jira Statuses by Project for transition IDs).

### Phase 5: Review (all tiers)

Invokes `engineer-toolkit:author-review`. The skill internally:

1. Runs `dotnet build` and `dotnet test` against the affected solution.
2. Delegates to `engineer-toolkit:code-reviewer` agent (always).
3. Delegates to `engineer-toolkit:architect-review` agent (Complex changes only; mapped from `Tier: Full`).
4. Produces a structured review summary with risk score, cost-of-change, and findings — designed for direct paste into PR description.

Build/test failures here are blocking. Critical findings should be addressed before opening PR.

`superpowers:verification-before-completion` is the discipline that applies throughout Phase 4 — Phase 5 is the formal gate before PR.

### Phase 6: Wrap

1. Commit, push, open PR via `gh pr create` — body sourced from author-review summary.
2. Update issue file: `Status: Code Review`, `PR: <link>`.
3. Clear `Mode: direct` if it was set.
4. Discussion entry: `[agent] Implementation complete. PR: <link>. Author-review: <risk-score>/10, cost: <reversible/moderate/irreversible>.`

User reviews PR in Azure DevOps; after merge, user transitions Jira (typically to "Development Complete"). On next `/work` cycle, Phase 2 picks up the Jira change and Phase 3 sweeps the folder to `Complete\`.

### Archive

After 30 days in `Complete\`: move to `Archive\`. (Unchanged from before.)

## Agent Workflow

Workflow phases invoke superpowers skills as their primary mechanism. Legacy agents (`fullstack-planning`, `Plan`, `prompt-engineer`, the Phase 1.5 plan-cleanup `code-reviewer` step) are removed.

### Spec phase (Full tier)

| Skill | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `superpowers:brainstorming` | Iterative Q&A to produce a spec at `Active\<KEY>\spec.md`. |

Tools available inside brainstorming for research:
- `Explore` agent (codebase discovery)
- `general-purpose` agent (catch-all)

### Plan phase (Standard + Full tiers)

| Skill | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `superpowers:writing-plans` | Convert spec (or issue description directly, for Standard tier) into bite-sized tasks with file paths, exact code, and verification steps. Output saved to `Active\<KEY>\plan.md`. |

### Execute phase

| Mode | Skill | Notes |
|------|-------|-------|
| Direct (`/direct`) | `superpowers:subagent-driven-development` | Spawns one sub-subagent per task in the current chat. User reviews between tasks. |
| Async (`/work`) | `superpowers:executing-plans` | Dispatched subagent runs plan in a separate session with internal review checkpoints. |

`superpowers:verification-before-completion` is the discipline applied throughout Execute — never claim a task complete without running the verification command from the plan and confirming the expected output.

For .NET work specifically: always run `dotnet build <solution>` after edits, before claiming the task done.

### Review phase

| Skill | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `engineer-toolkit:author-review` | Pre-PR self-review. Runs build/tests, delegates to `code-reviewer` (always) and `architect-review` (Complex only), produces structured summary for PR description. |

Optional, on demand only (not auto-run):
| Skill | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `engineer-toolkit:reviewer-check` | Post-PR independent reviewer pass. Validates author-review output and adds its own findings as a PR comment. |

### Tier → Complexity mapping

`engineer-toolkit:author-review` distinguishes Simple vs Complex changes. Map workflow tier:

| Workflow Tier | author-review Complexity |
|---------------|--------------------------|
| Trivial | Simple |
| Standard | Simple |
| Full | Complex |

